  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 308 PM EDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\n*****************Warm and Sunny Today*********************\n\nLexington and Louisville have officially broken the daily high \ntemperature records at 89. Other daily high temperature records \n(Bowling Green) are on the verge of being broken by a degree or two. \nSunny skies continue throughout today and breaking those record \nhighs isn't out of the question entirely yet. Southwest winds at 8-\n12 mph persist overnight, bringing warmer dew points from gulf \nmoisture and a plume of moist air into Louisville's area by early \ntomorrow morning. Overnight lows with the added moisture are again \nright on the cusp of breaking the warm min records right around 70. \n\n*************Tomorrow and Severe Potential***************\n\nTomorrow brings clear skies for S Central KY and overcast conditions \nfor Southern Indiana at dawn. By noon, most of the CWA will be \ncloudy. Showers and pockets of thunderstorms should follow \napproximately the same timing. Ahead of the warm front pushing \nthrough, we will see enough surface heating to contribute to surface \ndestabilization started by those high overnight temps and dew \npoints. Expect to see highs in the upper 80s/low 90s tomorrow with \ndew points in the low 70s throughout the day. The entire CWA except \nfor the southeast corner is in a marginal risk tomorrow for severe \nweather, mainly a wind-driven threat. Additionally, all of the CWA \nis in at least a marginal risk for excessive rainfall, with the \nBowling Green area, central KY, the Ohio River Valley, and Southern \nIndiana in a slight risk for excessive rainfall. These severe risks \nare based on the heavy moisture-loading occurring, with PWATs in the \n2-2.5\" range across the CWA. Much of the wind-driven severe will \nlikely be as these storms collapse; there isn't enough shear to hold \na cell together, so storms will likely grow due to seasonally-high \ninstability, dump rain, and then collapse in a summer-like \nprecipitation pattern. This should remain relatively consistent \nalong the front, so expect heavy lightning activity, locally high \nrainfall values (generally .5-1.5\" with local values in the 2-4\"), \nlocalized flash flooding, and a few occurences of winds in the 50-60 \nmph range tomorrow. \n\nRain will continue through the overnight hours across the CWA, but \nshould be lighter in intensity and more consistent across the \nregion. In regions that may see a few breaks from stratiform-type \nrain, fog will likely develop due to light winds and low dew point \ndepressions. As the front pushes back north on Thursday morning, S \nIndiana will see rain the longest and clips a marginal severe \noutlook for the moment. This is again likely wind-driven and will be \nlimited by the amount of cloud cover. If clouds can clear out faster \nthan noon, severe weather potential may increase but is highly \nunconfident at the moment. Daytime highs should be in the upper 80s \nfor S Kentucky and low 80s for S Indiana and the Ohio Valley, again \ndependent upon cloud cover. Another muggy day, with dew points in \nthe low 70s and upper 60s in S Kentucky. Overnight lows in the upper \n50s/low 60s.\n\n*****************Great Weekend Ahead***********************\n\nFriday breaks out the fall-like weather again, with highs in the low \nto mid-60s; a cold front makes it's way through, bringing gusty \nwinds in the 30 mph range and showers with it. Post-frontal \novernight lows push the upper-30s and lower-40s mark, with frost in \nan isolated valley entirely possible as winds calm and the boundary \nlayer decouples. \n\nThis weekend will see crisp, sunny highs in the upper-50s to mid-60s \nand nights perfect for bonfire season with lows in the low- to mid-\n40s. Early next week starts to warm up toward the upper 60s and \nlower 70s, with overnight lows in the 50 degree range.",
